1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:05,371
Hi — thank you for the opportunity to walk you through Lebenslauf Boost AI.

2
00:00:05,371 --> 00:00:11,368
This is a full-stack AI engineering project I built to optimize a real résumé against a

3
00:00:11,368 --> 00:00:12,816
specific job posting.

4
00:00:12,816 --> 00:00:18,607
In this video I'll explain how the frontend and backend work, which external APIs we

5
00:00:18,607 --> 00:00:24,398
call and why, how the prompt system is designed, and how the full pipeline runs from

6
00:00:24,398 --> 00:00:25,570
upload to export.

7
00:00:26,070 --> 00:00:30,749
At a high level, the product solves a practical hiring problem.

8
00:00:30,749 --> 00:00:36,987
Candidates need their résumé rewritten for each job, but unconstrained generative AI

9
00:00:36,987 --> 00:00:39,734
often invents skills or achievements.

10
00:00:39,734 --> 00:00:45,750
Our design principle is truthfulness first: the model may only use facts from the

11
00:00:45,750 --> 00:00:46,938
uploaded CV.

12
00:00:46,938 --> 00:00:53,102
We retrieve the most relevant CV sections with RAG, generate one or two drafts with

13
00:00:53,102 --> 00:00:59,414
Claude and OpenAI, score them against the job keywords, let the user refine the text,

14
00:00:59,414 --> 00:01:03,944
and export a polished PDF or Word file in one of six designs.

15
00:01:04,444 --> 00:01:08,664
Architecturally, I kept the system intentionally modular.

16
00:01:08,664 --> 00:01:15,180
The frontend is a lightweight vanilla JavaScript SPA — no React build step — styled in a

17
00:01:15,180 --> 00:01:21,696
custom Sapphire Nightfall design system, with Boosti, an animated guide that walks users

18
00:01:21,696 --> 00:01:22,955
through the flow.

19
00:01:22,955 --> 00:01:27,545
The backend is FastAPI with thin routers and thicker services.

20
00:01:27,545 --> 00:01:33,617
LLM access goes through a provider abstraction so Claude and OpenAI share the same

21
00:01:33,617 --> 00:01:34,802
interface.

22
00:01:34,802 --> 00:01:40,873
Prompt text lives outside the code as German and English template files, so prompt

23
00:01:40,873 --> 00:01:44,353
engineering can evolve without touching Python.

24
00:01:44,353 --> 00:01:46,945
SQLite stores sessions and history.

25
00:01:46,945 --> 00:01:53,090
And when keys or embeddings are missing, the app degrades gracefully into a clearly

26
00:01:53,090 --> 00:01:55,830
labeled demo mode instead of failing.

27
00:01:56,330 --> 00:02:00,619
On the frontend, the experience is a three-step wizard.

28
00:02:00,619 --> 00:02:07,480
Step one collects the job description, optional wishes, the CV file, the provider choice

29
00:02:07,480 --> 00:02:11,847
— Claude, OpenAI, or compare — and the prompt technique.

30
00:02:11,847 --> 00:02:18,631
Step two is the editing surface: the generated markdown, a compare bar when both models

31
00:02:18,631 --> 00:02:24,245
run, an ATS keyword panel, and refine chips like shorter or more formal.

32
00:02:24,245 --> 00:02:28,690
Step three is design and download with six visual themes.

33
00:02:28,690 --> 00:02:35,551
API keys follow a bring-your-own-key model: they live only in localStorage, are attached

34
00:02:35,551 --> 00:02:39,684
to each request, and are never persisted server-side.

35
00:02:39,684 --> 00:02:45,844
All network calls go through a small api.js module with timeouts on generation.

36
00:02:46,344 --> 00:02:50,594
On the backend, I separated HTTP from business logic.

37
00:02:50,594 --> 00:02:56,448
Routers handle sessions, document upload, generation, refine, and export.

38
00:02:56,448 --> 00:03:03,265
Services own the real work: file extraction from PDF, DOCX, or text, RAG indexing and

39
00:03:03,265 --> 00:03:08,317
retrieval, generation orchestration, and PDF or Word rendering.

40
00:03:08,317 --> 00:03:14,732
The LLM package defines an abstract provider, then concrete Anthropic and OpenAI

41
00:03:14,732 --> 00:03:21,709
implementations that both return a structured result with content, model name, and demo

42
00:03:21,709 --> 00:03:22,992
flag.

43
00:03:22,992 --> 00:03:25,638
Pydantic validates every request.

44
00:03:25,638 --> 00:03:32,535
A session is the hub: at most one CV document, many generations, and a message history

45
00:03:32,535 --> 00:03:33,818
used for refine.

46
00:03:34,318 --> 00:03:40,629
We use two different text-generation APIs, which was an explicit project requirement.

47
00:03:40,629 --> 00:03:46,939
For Claude, we call Anthropic's Messages API with a system prompt and a message list.

48
00:03:46,939 --> 00:03:53,398
For OpenAI, we call Chat Completions with the same logical roles, and separately we use

49
00:03:53,398 --> 00:03:59,041
OpenAI embeddings — text-embedding-3-small — to vectorize CV chunks for RAG.

50
00:03:59,041 --> 00:04:05,574
Key resolution is important for privacy and demos: a key typed in the UI wins, otherwise

51
00:04:05,574 --> 00:04:12,107
we fall back to a server environment key, and if neither exists we return a labeled demo

52
00:04:12,107 --> 00:04:15,374
résumé so the full product flow still works.

53
00:04:15,374 --> 00:04:21,833
In compare mode, both providers run independently; we do not ask one model to judge the

54
00:04:21,833 --> 00:04:23,021
other.

55
00:04:23,021 --> 00:04:27,624
Instead we rank drafts with our own ATS keyword overlap score.

56
00:04:28,124 --> 00:04:32,703
The prompt system is one of the parts I'm most intentional about.

57
00:04:32,703 --> 00:04:38,691
There are twelve template files under the prompts folder — six logical roles, each in

58
00:04:38,691 --> 00:04:44,468
German and English: system role, few-shot examples, chain-of-thought instructions,

59
00:04:44,468 --> 00:04:47,991
output format, the user message shell, and refine.

60
00:04:47,991 --> 00:04:53,274
Role prompting sets a senior executive résumé writer who must stay factual.

61
00:04:53,274 --> 00:04:59,192
Few-shot shows weak versus strong bullets so the model learns impact writing without

62
00:04:59,192 --> 00:05:00,460
inventing numbers.

63
00:05:00,460 --> 00:05:06,237
Chain-of-thought forces a private seven-step analysis — job requirements, evidence

64
00:05:06,237 --> 00:05:11,028
mapping, keyword plan — but those steps are never shown to the user.

65
00:05:11,028 --> 00:05:14,339
Format constraints lock the markdown structure.

66
00:05:14,339 --> 00:05:20,397
And dynamic context injection fills the user message with the job posting, wishes, and

67
00:05:20,397 --> 00:05:21,947
retrieved CV excerpts.

68
00:05:21,947 --> 00:05:28,006
When technique is set to auto, we combine few-shot and chain-of-thought in one prompt.

69
00:05:28,506 --> 00:05:31,632
Here is the generation pipeline in order.

70
00:05:31,632 --> 00:05:35,519
First, upload extracts text and optionally a photo.

71
00:05:35,519 --> 00:05:41,923
Second, we chunk the CV — about seven hundred characters with overlap — and build an

72
00:05:41,923 --> 00:05:48,326
index with embeddings when an OpenAI key is available, otherwise pure-Python TF-IDF.

73
00:05:48,326 --> 00:05:54,958
Third, at generate time we retrieve the top chunks most relevant to the job description

74
00:05:54,958 --> 00:05:56,178
and wishes.

75
00:05:56,178 --> 00:06:02,505
Fourth, prompts.py assembles the system persona, the selected technique blocks, the

76
00:06:02,505 --> 00:06:05,783
format rules, and the injected RAG context.

77
00:06:05,783 --> 00:06:12,416
Fifth, we call one or both providers, run ATS keyword analysis against the job posting,

78
00:06:12,416 --> 00:06:17,828
and persist each draft plus the conversation messages for later refine.

79
00:06:18,328 --> 00:06:22,250
After the first draft, refine uses conversation history.

80
00:06:22,250 --> 00:06:28,063
We send the previous user message, the assistant draft, and a new instruction — for

81
00:06:28,063 --> 00:06:34,086
example make it shorter or more technical — then store a new generation with technique

82
00:06:34,086 --> 00:06:35,206
refine.

83
00:06:35,206 --> 00:06:38,918
Nothing is overwritten, so versions remain auditable.

84
00:06:38,918 --> 00:06:44,801
In compare mode the UI highlights the higher ATS score, but the user can pick either

85
00:06:44,801 --> 00:06:45,921
draft.

86
00:06:45,921 --> 00:06:52,014
Export is intentionally stateless: the client sends the final markdown, design, format,

87
00:06:52,014 --> 00:06:53,345
and optional photo.

88
00:06:53,345 --> 00:06:59,017
ReportLab and python-docx render six visual themes without writing files into the

89
00:06:59,017 --> 00:07:00,138
database.

90
00:07:00,638 --> 00:07:06,944
If I were summarizing engineering judgment for a hiring conversation, I'd highlight four

91
00:07:06,944 --> 00:07:08,090
things.

92
00:07:08,090 --> 00:07:10,885
Privacy: user keys stay in the browser.

93
00:07:10,885 --> 00:07:17,048
Reliability: demo mode means reviewers can exercise the full flow without credentials.

94
00:07:17,048 --> 00:07:23,067
Evaluation: we measure fit with an application-specific ATS keyword score, not vague

95
00:07:23,067 --> 00:07:24,214
vibes.

96
00:07:24,214 --> 00:07:30,090
And maintainability: prompts are data, providers are swappable, routers stay thin.

97
00:07:30,090 --> 00:07:35,464
The honest next steps for production would be PostgreSQL with Alembic, real

98
00:07:35,464 --> 00:07:40,480
authentication, broader automated tests, and containerized deployment.

99
00:07:40,980 --> 00:07:47,299
That's the system end to end: a product-shaped AI workflow with clear architecture, two

100
00:07:47,299 --> 00:07:53,400
real model providers, a structured prompt stack, retrieval grounding, and a complete

101
00:07:53,400 --> 00:07:55,942
user journey from upload to export.

102
00:07:55,942 --> 00:08:01,607
Happy to go deeper on any layer — prompts, RAG, or the FastAPI service design.

103
00:08:01,607 --> 00:08:03,278
Thank you for watching.
